Summary
The visual analysis of WallStreetBets posts from 2020–2021 highlights several key patterns in retail investor attention and discussion behavior.
First, engagement on the platform is highly uneven. From the distribution of post scores and number of comments, most articles have limited interaction, while a few posrs have extremely high attention. This suggests that retail investors tend to collectively follow a limited set of highly visible discussions, suggesting that investment interest is driven by a few prominent topics rather than being evenly distributed across posts.
Second, there is a strong positive relationship between post scores and comment activity. Posts with higher voting scores also tend to generate more discussion, indicating that investor attention tends to cluster: when a topic becomes popular, it attracts visibility and active participation. This pattern reflects the collective and social nature of investment decisions within the community.
Third, temporal patterns suggest that investor discussions are influenced by time and external events. Engagement varies across hours of the day and days of the week, suggesting that postinh time can affect visibility and interaction. Moreover, the fluctuations in average engagement over the period under study, especially the noticeable peaks in early 2021, coincide with a period of increased retail investor activity in financial markets. This suggests that real-world market events strongly influence the intensity of online discussions and investment focus.
Finally, the dominance of text and image posts suggests that retail investors share ideas and react to opportunities primarily through short discussions and visual content, rather than long-form posts or external media. This highlights the fast-paced and conversational nature of investment communication in the WallStreetBets community.
Overall, the visual evidence suggests that retail investor attention to WallStreetBets over the 2020-2021 period was shaped by collective engagement dynamics, time effects, and external market events, rather than by simple structural features of individual posts.

